#%PAM-1.0
# The sddm 0.21.0-7 policy with a fingerprint path prepended to the auth stack.
# Everything from `account` down is upstream's file verbatim.
#
# The two factors are serial here, not concurrent. pam_fprintd(8) LIMITATIONS: "The
# PAM stack is by design a serialised authentication, so it is not possible for
# pam_fprintd to allow authentication through passwords and fingerprints at the same
# time. It is up to the application using the PAM services to implement separate PAM
# processes and run separate authentication stacks separately." Quickshell does run
# two — hence quickshell-lock and quickshell-fprint — and sddm runs one. So the
# factor is chosen by what gets typed: a password, or an empty field and Enter.
#
# success=1 skips exactly the pam_fprintd line, which puts a password login back on
# the ordinary stack via the include — pam_shells, pam_nologin, faillock preauth,
# pam_unix reusing the token already collected, then faillock authsucc. That last
# module is why only the password path clears a lockout tally; a finger returns from
# `sufficient` before the include is ever reached, and so resets nothing (it
# increments nothing either — faillock's authfail is equally unreachable).
#
# The same short-circuit means a finger also bypasses pam_nologin and pam_shells.
# Accepted: neither is load-bearing at a display manager, and both are the hazards
# quickshell-lock avoids by including system-auth rather than login.
#
# max-tries=1 bounds the greeter. pam_fprintd defaults to 3 tries at a 30s timeout
# each, and sddm gives the user no way to abort a conversation in flight, so the
# default strands the login screen for 90 seconds on a finger that will not read.
# One try instead falls through to the include and returns the password field.
#
# ArchWiki SDDM warns that KWallet cannot be unlocked by a fingerprint. It does not
# apply here: kwallet-pam is not installed and neither pam_kwallet5.so nor
# pam_gnome_keyring.so exists on this machine, so the four keyring lines below are
# inert — kwallet arrives only as a transitive dependency of kio and
# plasma-workspace, and no wallet has ever been created. Installing a Secret Service
# provider later would give this file a real cost and is the point to revisit it.

auth        [success=1 new_authtok_reqd=1 default=ignore] pam_unix.so try_first_pass likeauth nullok
auth        sufficient  pam_fprintd.so          max-tries=1
auth        include     system-login
-auth       optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so
-auth       optional    pam_kwallet5.so

account     include     system-login

password    include     system-login
-password   optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so    use_authtok

session     optional    pam_keyinit.so          force revoke
session     include     system-login
-session    optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so    auto_start
-session    optional    pam_kwallet5.so         auto_start
